  "turnsGreenIn": "S15",
  "summary": "A Word table is one table across the reader's page breaks: its rows carry on where the page broke them, row 3 after rows 1 and 2. The Word reader stacks every cell, empty ones too, so a short table can run past its page (#157, the corpus's `matprotokoll`), and a long one crosses pages in Word itself. A table of numbered rows (S3c) counts all three, and a grid (S2c) keeps its third row, though each ended on a page of its own.",
